Pod root-cause report for workload {{user.w}} in rca-demo
Evidence:
- pod {{result.0.pods.0.name}} on node {{result.1.pod.node}}: phase {{result.1.pod.phase}}, restarts {{result.0.pods.0.restarts}}, reason {{result.0.pods.0.reason}}
- container {{result.1.pod.containers.0.name}}: last state terminated, reason {{result.1.pod.containers.0.last_state.reason}}, exit code {{result.1.pod.containers.0.last_state.exit_code}}
- memory limit of the container: {{result.1.pod.containers.0.resources.limits.memory}}
- log line 1 of {{result.3.line_count}}: {{result.3.untrusted_text.lines.0}}
- workload deployment {{result.4.workload.name}}: desired {{result.4.workload.desired}}, ready {{result.4.workload.ready}}
Likely cause: out of memory. The container was killed with reason OOMKilled (exit code 137) because it used more memory than its limit of {{result.1.pod.containers.0.resources.limits.memory}}, and it restarts in a loop.
Confidence: high. The kubelet records the reason itself.

Recommended actions (for a person; I changed nothing and I have no write tools):
1. Find out whether the program really needs more memory or leaks it.
2. If it needs more, raise the memory limit and request in the deployment {{user.w}} through your normal change process; if it leaks, fix the program.
3. Watch the restart count after the change.

Pod root-cause report for workload {{user.w}} in rca-demo
Evidence:
- pod {{result.0.pods.0.name}} on node {{result.1.pod.node}}: phase {{result.1.pod.phase}}, ready {{result.0.pods.0.ready}}, container {{result.1.pod.containers.0.name}} state {{result.1.pod.containers.0.state.state}}, reason {{result.1.pod.containers.0.state.reason}}
- message of the waiting state: {{result.1.pod.containers.0.state.untrusted_text}}
- environment variable names of the container: {{result.1.pod.containers.0.env}}
- newest event: {{result.2.events.0.type}} {{result.2.events.0.reason}}
- workload deployment {{result.3.workload.name}}: desired {{result.3.workload.desired}}, ready {{result.3.workload.ready}}
Likely cause: missing configuration key. The container cannot be created because an environment variable takes its value from a ConfigMap key that the ConfigMap does not have (CreateContainerConfigError). The message above names the key and the ConfigMap.
Confidence: high. The kubelet names the missing key.

Recommended actions (for a person; I changed nothing and I have no write tools):
1. Compare the key named in the message with the keys of that ConfigMap (a person can read it; I cannot read ConfigMap values).
2. Add the key to the ConfigMap, or correct the key name in the deployment {{user.w}}, through your normal change process.
3. The pod starts by itself once the key exists.

